  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Most landlord and tenant relationships go smoothly. Rent gets paid, repairs get made and everyone moves on. But when something goes wrong, such as a broken furnace in January or a deposit that does not come back, it helps to know what Ohio law actually says. Ohio's landlord-tenant law is found in Ohio Revised Code (ORC) chapter 5321, and it applies to residential rentals across the state, including apartments in West Carrollton and the rest of Montgomery County. This guide gives a plain-English overview of the key points.</p>
<blockquote><strong>This is not legal advice.</strong> Laws change and every situation is different. For help with a specific problem, contact a licensed Ohio attorney or a local legal aid organization.</blockquote>

<h2>Your landlord's main obligations</h2>
<p>ORC 5321.04 lists the landlord's core duties. In general terms, a landlord must:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Follow building, housing, health and safety codes</strong> that materially affect health and safety.</li>
<li><strong>Make repairs</strong> and do what is reasonably necessary to keep the premises fit and habitable.</li>
<li><strong>Keep common areas safe and sanitary</strong>, such as hallways, stairs and laundry rooms.</li>
<li><strong>Maintain supplied systems and appliances</strong> in good working order, including electrical, plumbing, heating, ventilation and air conditioning fixtures and appliances the landlord provides.</li>
<li><strong>Provide trash receptacles</strong> and arrange for removal in buildings with multiple units, where required.</li>
<li><strong>Supply running water and reasonable heat and hot water</strong>, except where the tenant controls those utilities through a direct connection.</li>
<li><strong>Give reasonable notice before entering</strong>, as described below.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Your responsibilities as a tenant</h2>
<p>ORC 5321.05 lists the tenant's duties. In general, a tenant must:</p>
<ul>
<li>Keep the part of the premises they occupy safe and sanitary.</li>
<li>Dispose of trash properly.</li>
<li>Keep plumbing fixtures as clean as their condition permits.</li>
<li>Use electrical and plumbing fixtures and appliances properly.</li>
<li>Not deliberately or negligently damage the premises, or let guests do so.</li>
<li>Not disturb neighbors' peaceful enjoyment of the premises.</li>
<li>Follow the lease and reasonable rules of the property.</li>
</ul>
<p>Paying rent on time is, of course, central. Many of your rights, including the rent escrow process, depend on you being current on rent.</p>

<h2>Landlord entry and privacy</h2>
<p>Under ORC 5321.04(A)(8), a landlord must give reasonable notice of intent to enter and enter only at reasonable times, except in an emergency. Twenty-four hours' notice is presumed reasonable unless there is evidence to the contrary. In turn, ORC 5321.05 says a tenant should not unreasonably withhold consent for the landlord to enter to inspect, make repairs or show the unit. Your lease may add details about how notice is given.</p>

<h2>Security deposits</h2>
<p>ORC 5321.16 covers security deposits. Key points:</p>
<ul>
<li>When the lease ends, the landlord may apply the deposit to unpaid rent and to damages caused by the tenant's failure to meet their obligations.</li>
<li>Any deductions must be itemized and delivered in writing, along with any amount due back to the tenant, within 30 days after the lease ends and the tenant gives back possession.</li>
<li>The tenant should provide a forwarding address in writing. This matters if the landlord fails to return the deposit properly.</li>
<li>If a landlord wrongfully withholds part of a deposit, the tenant may be able to recover that amount plus damages equal to it and reasonable attorney's fees.</li>
<li>Certain larger deposits held for six months or more must earn interest under the same section.</li>
</ul>
<p>The best protection is documentation: dated photos at move-in and move-out, a completed inspection checklist and copies of everything in writing. Our <a href="/blog/renting-an-apartment-checklist/">renting an apartment checklist</a> covers move-in documentation.</p>

<!--products-->

<h2>When repairs are not made: rent escrow</h2>
<p>Ohio gives tenants a formal remedy when a landlord does not fix serious problems. Under ORC 5321.07, the general process is:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Give written notice</strong> to the landlord, or the landlord's agent, describing the problem.</li>
<li><strong>Allow a reasonable time</strong> for the repair, considering how serious it is, and not more than 30 days.</li>
<li><strong>If it is not fixed</strong>, the tenant may deposit rent with the clerk of the municipal or county court that has jurisdiction, apply to the court for an order to make repairs or reduce rent, or end the rental agreement.</li>
</ol>
<p>Important limits apply. You must be current on rent when you begin the process, and some small landlords may be exempt if they meet conditions in the statute and have given proper written notice. Simply stopping rent payments without following the process can lead to eviction. Talk to a lawyer or legal aid office before using rent escrow.</p>

<h2>Protection from retaliation and lockouts</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Retaliation:</strong> ORC 5321.02 generally prohibits a landlord from raising rent, cutting services or threatening eviction because a tenant complained to a government agency about code violations, complained to the landlord about the landlord's obligations, or joined a tenant organization.</li>
<li><strong>Self-help evictions:</strong> ORC 5321.15 prohibits a landlord from locking a tenant out or cutting off utilities to force them out. Eviction must go through the courts.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Ending a month-to-month tenancy</h2>
<p>For a month-to-month arrangement, ORC 5321.17 generally requires at least 30 days' notice before the periodic rental date to terminate. A fixed-term lease, such as a 12-month lease, follows its own terms, so read your lease carefully, especially the renewal and early termination sections.</p>

<h2>Practical tips for Ohio renters</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Put requests in writing.</strong> Email or a dated letter creates a record. Keep copies.</li>
<li><strong>Document conditions.</strong> Photos and video, with dates, at move-in, during problems and at move-out.</li>
<li><strong>Read the whole lease.</strong> Many disputes come down to a clause someone did not notice.</li>
<li><strong>Carry renters insurance.</strong> Your landlord's insurance generally does not cover your belongings.</li>
<li><strong>Stay current on rent.</strong> It protects your ability to use the remedies the law provides.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are still looking for a place, browse <a href="/apartments/">West Carrollton apartments</a> and ask each property how they handle maintenance requests and deposits before you sign.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>Ohio law gives renters meaningful protections around repairs, privacy, deposits and retaliation, along with clear responsibilities to keep the unit in good shape and pay rent on time. Knowing the basics of ORC chapter 5321 helps you solve problems early and calmly. When an issue is serious, get advice from a qualified Ohio attorney or legal aid office. This article is general information, not legal advice.</p>
